SELECT e1.employee_id, e1.name, count(*) as reports_count, ROUND(AVG(e2.age)) as average_age
FROM Employees e1
JOIN Employees e2
ON
e1.employee_id = e2.reports_to
GROUP BY e1.employee_id,e1.name
ORDER BY  e1.employee_id;


Note:
Why Do We Divide the Table into e1 and e2?
We need to differentiate between managers and their direct reports.
If we used only one table, we wouldn't be able to match managers with their reports.
e1 (Managers) links to e2 (Direct Reports) through reports_to.
Grouping allows us to count and average reports per manager.
COUNT(*) tells us how many employees report to e1.
AVG(e2.age) calculates the average age of employees reporting to e1.

Why COUNT(*)? (better option)
This is counting all rows where the e1.employee_id (the manager) is joined with their reports from e2 (employees who report to the manager). It counts the number of employees (e2.employee_id) who are reporting to the manager (e1.employee_id).
COUNT(*) counts all the rows returned by the join for each manager. It includes every row, regardless of the specific data in each column.
It effectively counts how many employees (rows) are reporting to a specific manager.

Can We Use e2.employee_id Instead?
yes, COUNT(e2.employee_id) counts only non-NULL values in the e2.employee_id column. If there's a situation where a manager has no employees reporting to them (i.e., no matching rows in the join), it won't count anything because e2.employee_id would be NULL.

WHy SELF JOINS?
✅ Self-joins allow us to match employees with their managers.
✅ Dividing the table into e1 (Managers) and e2 (Reports) enables aggregation per manager.
✅ Grouping by e1.employee_id ensures correct count and average age calculation.
